  rather than a signature tweak.

  First question, and it's the load-bearing one: everything about persistence and sharing depends on who mints the
  identifier.

  Right now [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m ([38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m) is a stub with a comment saying a real app would POST to it, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m fabricates
  [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m locally. So there is no server handing back an ID today.

  - [1mServer-issued[0m — the real backend authenticates and returns the canonical user ID. The only version that's
    trustworthy as identity, and the only one that can be the same ID your database uses. But it means the design
    depends on an endpoint that doesn't exist yet, so I'd build against a defined response contract and keep the stub
    until the backend lands.
  - [1mClient-generated[0m — mint an ID in the browser at login. Works today with no backend, but it identifies a browser, not
    a person: it can be forged, and it won't match any server-side record. Fine for analytics, wrong for anything
    authorization-shaped.
  - [1mDerived from the username[0m — deterministic, no backend, stable across devices. But usernames change, and it leaks the
    username into every place the ID travels.

  This choice is expensive to reverse: once other forms are reading the stored ID, changing what it [3mis[0m means touching
  all of them and invalidating whatever you've already recorded. My recommendation is server-issued, designed against a
  contract now even though the endpoint is a stub — it's the only option that survives contact with a real backend.
